The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (stop_capture)
    snd_pcm_stop(dpcm_capt->substream, SNDRV_PCM_STATE_DRAINING);
```
and in `free_cable`:
```c
loopback->cables[substream->number][dev] = NULL;
kfree(cable);
```
Reason:  
The `snd_pcm_stop()` call uses the peer substream pointer (`dpcm_capt->substream`) obtained while holding `cable->lock`, but the lock is released before the call. A concurrent `free_cable()` (e.g., triggered by closing the capture substream) can clear the stream entry and free the cable (including the runtime and `loopback_pcm`) while `snd_pcm_stop()` still accesses that memory, causing a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// Inside loopback_check_format, under cable->lock:
atomic_inc(&cable->stop_count);
stop_capture = true;
// After snd_pcm_stop:
if (atomic_dec_and_test(&cable->stop_count))
    wake_up(&cable->stop_wait);
```
and in `free_cable`:
```c
wait_event(cable->stop_wait, !atomic_read(&cable->stop_count));
```
and in `loopback_open`:
```c
atomic_set(&cable->stop_count, 0);
init_waitqueue_head(&cable->stop_wait);
```
Reason:  
The `stop_count` atomic variable tracks the number of in-flight peer stops. By incrementing it before dropping `cable->lock` and decrementing after `snd_pcm_stop()` returns, we ensure that `free_cable()` will wait (via `wait_event`) until all ongoing stops finish before freeing the cable. The `wake_up()` call signals any waiters when the last stop completes. This explicit synchronization prevents the use-after-free.

